{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"EXT_ID:18026577 AND SRC:MED","internalQuery":"EXT_ID:18026577 AND SRC:MED","resultType":"CORE","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"18026577","source":"MED","pmid":"18026577","pmcid":"PMC2658588","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC2658588"]},"doi":"10.1155/2007/720205","title":"Effect of a fermented milk combining Lactobacillus acidophilus Cl1285 and Lactobacillus casei in the prevention of antibiotic-associated diarrhea: a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial.","authorString":"Beausoleil M, Fortier N, Guénette S, L'ecuyer A, Savoie M, Franco M, Lachaine J, Weiss K.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Beausoleil M","lastName":"Beausoleil","firstName":"M","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy, University of Montreal, Montreal, Canada. mbeausoleil.hmr@ssss.gouv.qc.ca"}]}},{"fullName":"Fortier N","lastName":"Fortier","firstName":"N","initials":"N"},{"fullName":"Guénette S","lastName":"Guénette","firstName":"S","initials":"S"},{"fullName":"L'ecuyer A","lastName":"L'ecuyer","firstName":"A","initials":"A"},{"fullName":"Savoie M","lastName":"Savoie","firstName":"M","initials":"M"},{"fullName":"Franco M","lastName":"Franco","firstName":"M","initials":"M"},{"fullName":"Lachaine J","lastName":"Lachaine","firstName":"J","initials":"J"},{"fullName":"Weiss K","lastName":"Weiss","firstName":"K","initials":"K"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"11","volume":"21","journalIssueId":1481383,"dateOfPublication":"2007 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2007,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Can J Gastroenterol","title":"Canadian journal of gastroenterology = Journal canadien de gastroenterologie","essn":"1916-7237","issn":"0835-7900","isoabbreviation":"Can J Gastroenterol","nlmid":"8807867"},"printPublicationDate":"2007-11-01"},"pubYear":"2007","pageInfo":"732-736","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Antibiotic-associated diarrhea is an important problem in hospitalized patients. The use of probiotics is gaining interest in the scientific community as a potential measure to prevent this complication. The main objective of the present study was to assess the efficacy and safety of a fermented milk combining Lactobacillus acidophilus and Lactobacillus casei that is widely available in Canada, in the prevention of antibiotic-associated diarrhea.<h4>Methods</h4>In this double-blind, randomized study, hospitalized patients were randomly assigned to receive either a lactobacilli-fermented milk or a placebo on a daily basis.<h4>Results</h4>Among 89 randomized patients, antibiotic-associated diarrhea occurred in seven of 44 patients (15.9%) in the lactobacilli group and in 16 of 45 patients (35.6%) in the placebo group (OR 0.34, 95% CI 0.125 to 0.944; P=0.05). The median hospitalization duration was eight days in the lactobacilli group, compared with 10 days in the placebo group (P=0.09). Overall, the lactobacilli-fermented milk was well tolerated.<h4>Conclusion</h4>The daily administration of a lactobacilli-fermented milk was safe and effective in the prevention of antibiotic-associated diarrhea in hospitalized patients.","affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy, University of Montreal, Montreal, Canada. mbeausoleil.hmr@ssss.gouv.qc.ca","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Randomized Controlled Trial","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Milk","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"MI","qualifierName":"microbiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Lactobacillus acidophilus"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descripto